# Test: the initial-sync End-of-RIB does NOT wait for a route pushed through the
#       RAW rail, and that route still reaches the wire
#
# VALIDATES: RFC 4724 Section 4 -- "The End-of-RIB marker MUST be sent by a BGP
#            speaker to its peer once it completes the initial routing update
#            (including the case when there is no update to send) for an address
#            family after the BGP session is established". That update is ZE'S
#            OWN TABLE: a process which creates routes stands where a peer
#            stands, and ze never holds its own marker for a peer's (owner
#            ruling, 2026-09-18, superseding 2026-08-30). The marker therefore
#            comes FIRST here, and the injected route is an ordinary update
#            after it, exactly as a peer's route would be.
#
# PREVENTS: the marker waiting on a process again. It used to, and the wait was
#           a window rather than a barrier: the queue gate closed and the
#           forwarding rail reopened before it while the marker was still owed,
#           so a route forwarded from another peer could already precede the
#           marker that claims to end the initial table. Which side a process's
#           own routes landed on then depended on a two-second timer, and four
#           functional tests asserting the old order lost that race under load.
#
# WHY RAW IS THE RAIL HERE. The attach block below grants `send [ raw ]` and no
# other send word, so this exercises the raw arm of the injection path. The
# assertion is that the route ARRIVES, after the marker, however it was injected.
#
# NON-VACUITY. The observer waits for ESTABLISHMENT rather than for eor-sent, so
# it does not take the marker as its go-ahead. Delete the raw arm of
# MayPushRoutes, or the `send [ raw ]` grant below, and the injected route never
# reaches the wire at all, which is the red this file exists to produce.

# ze-peer expectations
stdin=peer:terminator=EOF_PEER
option=tcp_connections:value=1
option=timeout:value=20s
# seq=1 -- the injected route. ORIGIN=IGP, AS_PATH empty (iBGP, both sides
# 65000), NEXT_HOP=10.0.1.254, LOCAL_PREF=200, NLRI 10.0.1.0/24.
#
# ze's own initial-sync marker is NOT asserted, because it no longer has a
# defined position against this route: it follows ze's own table and waits for
# no process, so it lands before or after the injection depending on whether
# that injection beat the queue gate closing. The checker only NOTES an early
# marker (checker.go, noteMisordered) rather than satisfying a later
# expectation with it, so neither order can be written here truthfully. What
# this file proves is that the RAW rail delivers the route at all.
expect=bgp:conn=1:seq=1:hex=FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF00300200000015400101004002004003040A0001FE400504000000C8180A0001
EOF_PEER

# ze bgp configuration
stdin=ze-bgp:terminator=EOF_CONF
plugin {
	external raw-injector {
		run "le test fixture plugin/initial-sync-barrier-raw"
		encoder json
	}
}
bgp {
	peer peer1 {
		connection {
			remote {
				ip 127.0.0.1
			}
			local {
				ip 127.0.0.1
				accept false
			}
		}
		session {
			asn {
				local 65000
				remote 65000
			}
			router-id 10.0.0.1
			family {
				ipv4/unicast { prefix { maximum 10000; } }
			}
		}
		behavior {
			group-updates disable
		}

		attach process raw-injector {
			send [ raw ]
			# The peer-up event, which is how this peer tells the process its
			# session started. A process the peer never tells cannot put a route
			# into that session's INITIAL routing update, so the barrier does not
			# wait for one (Peer.initialUpdateReporters). The send list is still
			# `raw` and nothing else, so the raw arm of MayPushRoutes is still the
			# only reason this process reaches the wire at all.
			receive [ state ]
		}
	}
}
EOF_CONF

# Process orchestration
cmd=background:seq=1:exec=le test peer --port $PORT:stdin=peer
cmd=foreground:seq=2:exec=ze -:stdin=ze-bgp:timeout=20s
expect=exit:code=0
reject=stderr:pattern=panic recovered
reject=stderr:pattern=ZE-OBSERVER-FAIL
